  <section class="section section--flush-top">
    <div class="wrap--narrow faq">
      <details open>
        <summary>What is the benefit of a permit expediter, and what is their role on a project?</summary>
        <div class="faq__a">
          <p><strong>Knowledge and focus.</strong> A building permit expediter understands local building codes, permit types and submission processes, and advises on the most efficient and economical order to meet requirements, especially on complex projects. Their only focus is securing the permit on time.</p>
          <p><strong>Time managed well.</strong> Expediters manage expectations for everyone and can estimate how long a permit or license will take. The project team can then coordinate contractors and other commitments, saving time and money.</p>
          <p><strong>A single permit manager.</strong> The expediter serves the architect, owner, building manager and general contractor as the single point of contact with the municipality, sending targeted updates to the team and consolidating deliverables for the city.</p>
          <p><strong>Economical.</strong> Instead of adding a code consultant and land use planner to research and review every submission cycle, these services are included in Burnham’s flat-rate price for most typical projects.</p>
          <p><strong>Concentrated experience.</strong> Expediters have worked on hundreds of projects across many states and jurisdictions. Someone familiar with the process and local officials is the fastest, most reliable way to get permits and licenses right the first time.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>When is it worth hiring a permit expediter?</summary>
        <div class="faq__a">
          <p>Start by researching directly with the municipality. If any of the following apply, a free call with Burnham’s team is worth it:</p>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>You have an important deadline or tight schedule.</li>
            <li>It is your first renovation or project and you are unsure whether you need formal approvals or a permit.</li>
            <li>You are unfamiliar with the municipality where you are building, or the project is complex.</li>
            <li>You are working with a new contractor or a team that has not worked together before.</li>
            <li>It is a multi-site rollout for a national brand, where coordination is critical.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How much faster will I get a permit?</summary>
        <div class="faq__a">
          <p>Every project is unique, but working with Burnham will be faster than learning as you go. The team has been there and done that a thousand times.</p>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>Most of the time saved comes from fewer review cycles with the city, by getting it right on the first submission.</li>
            <li>There are far fewer last-minute surprises that can set a project back weeks or months. Burnham looks at the whole project at the start, identifying needs such as street closures, signage or rezoning that can run in parallel instead of one after another.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How much does permit expediting cost?</summary>
        <div class="faq__a">
          <p>Burnham’s services are typically a flat rate, so you can budget precisely. Providers that bill hourly can expand scope unpredictably.</p>
          <p>Included in Burnham’s services: an in-house team of design and building code professionals with decades of experience, who can turn around changes the same day when project managers hit an issue, and a secure, cloud-based database with more than 20 years of contacts, municipal requirements from every jurisdiction Burnham has worked in, and the plans and documents it has submitted.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>I’ve already submitted for a permit and the city is slow. Can you help?</summary>
        <div class="faq__a">
          <p>When Burnham takes on a project mid-submission, there is a strong chance the process will need to restart with the city.</p>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>Restarting can be worthwhile if the scope or complexity grew mid-project, if factors such as zoning, building code concerns or historic landmark approvals were missed, or if the team is having difficulty producing required documents.</li>
            <li>As municipalities move online and adopt new software and approval workflows, some are occasionally backlogged in ways no one can control, and waiting is part of the process.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What types of projects do you take on, residential or commercial?</summary>
        <div class="faq__a">
          <p>Commercial projects make up most of Burnham’s work because they are larger, more complex and need more approvals, but residential projects are a large part of the business too. No project is too big or too small.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What projects require a permit?</summary>
        <div class="faq__a">
          <p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> a building permit is required for new construction, renovations, installing heating and cooling systems, and any plumbing or electrical work.</p>
          <p>Project types that often require permits include additions, basement work, carports, chimney work, conversion or deconversion of multi-unit buildings, outdoor decks, demolition, driveways, electrical and plumbing work, fencing, HVAC work, garages, gut rehabs, hot water heaters, masonry, security alarms and roofing.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Where do you run permits?</summary>
        <div class="faq__a">
          <p>All 50 states, Puerto Rico and Canada, with offices in <a class="link" href="/contact/">Chicago, New York, Los Angeles, San Francisco, San Jose, Denver, Dallas and Orlando</a>.</p>
          <p>If you have local expertise and would like to establish a Burnham office in your city, visit the <a class="link" href="/about/careers/">careers page</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What else should I ask Burnham to look at besides my permit?</summary>
        <div class="faq__a">
          <ul class="list">
            <li><strong>Zoning,</strong> before you build: can you do what you want where you want, and can that change? Confirm with the city that your business can operate at an address before you sign a lease.</li>
            <li><strong>Building code review,</strong> if you are renovating: Burnham’s <a class="link" href="/what-we-do/code-consulting/">code consulting group</a> can confirm the project is structurally sound and meets accessibility requirements.</li>
            <li><strong>Licenses and special inspections</strong> for food service, healthcare facilities, business operations, signage and seating capacity.</li>
            <li><strong>Environmental and health</strong> grinding permits.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do you handle permitting for data centers?</summary>
        <div class="faq__a">
          <p>Yes. Burnham supports greenfield and retrofit data center projects, from core and shell through tenant improvements and critical infrastructure upgrades. The team coordinates closely with your design, electrical and mechanical teams and the local authority having jurisdiction (AHJ) to align power, cooling, life safety and structural requirements, so permits move forward with fewer surprises.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

